    "updated_at": "2026-03-20T00:23:55Z",
    "body": "> It makes sense for initialize/upgrade to skip such subtrees.\n\nJust to clarify, is this the case you're thinking of?\n\n```html\n<div id=\"container\"><div id=\"child\" customelementregistry></div></div>\n<script>\n  const registry = new CustomElementRegistry();\n  registry.initialize(container);\n  console.assert(container.customElementRegistry === registry);\n  // what about the child?\n  console.assert(child.customElementRegistry === registry);\n</script>\n```\n\nI think the attribute is and must be lossy and therefore this should initialize child to have the registry.\n\nAnd specifically with respect to serialization, imo, we should not do something special and definitely *not* try to [replicate what happens](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/parsing.html#example-html-serializer-roundtrip-is-attribute) for the `is` attribute.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
